    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Most property owners get their insurer’s estimate as a PDF full of codes, abbreviations, and numbers, along with a check that may or may not match what a contractor has quoted. An <strong>insurance estimate review in Dallas</strong> breaks that document down into plain English: what was included, what was left out, how each item was measured and priced, and how the final payment was calculated. Dallas Public Adjuster reviews estimates for homeowners and business owners across Dallas County, from Irving and Carrollton to Mesquite and DeSoto, so they can make informed decisions before accepting a settlement.</p>

<h2>How to Read an Insurance Estimate</h2>
<p>Most adjuster estimates follow a similar structure. Knowing the sections makes the whole document easier to follow.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Header and claim information</td><td>Claim number, date of loss, type of loss, inspection date, and price list used</td></tr>
<tr><td>Line items by area</td><td>Each repair item grouped by room, elevation, or roof slope, with quantity, unit, and unit price</td></tr>
<tr><td>Replacement cost value (RCV)</td><td>The estimated cost to repair or replace with like kind and quality</td></tr>
<tr><td>Depreciation</td><td>The amount subtracted for age and condition, and whether it is recoverable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Actual cash value (ACV)</td><td>RCV minus depreciation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deductible</td><td>Your out-of-pocket share, which may be a percentage wind/hail deductible</td></tr>
<tr><td>Net claim and prior payments</td><td>What the insurer is paying now and what may be released later</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The price list code in the header matters. Estimating software like Xactimate uses regional pricing that is updated over time. If an estimate uses an outdated or mismatched price list, every line can be off.</p>

<h2>What We Look for in a Review</h2>
<h3>Scope: is everything there?</h3>
<p>We compare the estimate with the property itself. On Dallas roofs, that often means checking ridge caps, starter, drip edge, valleys, flashing, vents, pipe jacks, gutters, and downspouts, along with steep and high charges on taller homes. Inside, we look for missing drywall, insulation, texture, paint, flooring, baseboards, and cabinets. On commercial claims, we check membranes, insulation, edge metal, and rooftop units.</p>
<h3>Quantities: are the measurements right?</h3>
<p>Roof squares, waste factors, pitch, wall area, and flooring square footage are easy to understate. Small measurement errors multiply quickly across a large roof or a whole floor.</p>
<h3>Pricing: does it match local costs?</h3>
<p>Unit costs should reflect current Dallas-area labor and materials. After large storms, such as the June 12, 2023 hail near Cedar Hill or the May 28, 2024 windstorm in Garland and Richardson, demand for contractors can rise quickly.</p>
<h3>Repair vs. replacement</h3>
<p>Estimates sometimes call for spot repairs on materials that cannot reasonably be patched or matched. Your policy language and material availability both matter.</p>
<h3>Depreciation and deductibles</h3>
<p>We check whether depreciation is reasonable for the age and condition of materials, whether it is applied to labor, and whether the right deductible was used. See <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a>.</p>
<h3>Code items and O&amp;P</h3>
<p>We note items the local building department may require and whether general contractor overhead and profit fits the job.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Damage-Roof-Insurance-Claim-How-to-Protect-Your-Roof-3-1024x683.png" alt="Adjuster at a desk with a tablet showing roof photos next to a hard hat" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Matching photos to each line item makes it easier to see what an estimate includes and what it leaves out.</figcaption></figure>

[[cta]]

<h2>Comparing the Estimate With Contractor Bids</h2>
<p>A contractor bid that is much higher than the insurer’s estimate does not automatically mean the claim is underpaid, and it does not mean the bid is inflated either. The useful question is why the two differ. Is the contractor including items the insurer left out, using different quantities, or pricing materials differently? A single total is hard to compare. A line-item bid makes the differences clear. For roof claims, read <a href="/how-to-review-underpaid-roof-insurance-estimate/">how to review an underpaid roof insurance estimate</a>.</p>

<h2>What Happens After the Review</h2>
<p>A review gives you options rather than a fixed path. Depending on what it shows, you may:</p>
<ul>
<li>Accept the estimate if it reasonably reflects the damage.</li>
<li>Send the insurer a written request to add specific items with supporting photos.</li>
<li>Submit an <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplement</a> for damage found during repairs.</li>
<li>Pursue an <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claim</a> with a full competing estimate.</li>
<li>Consider <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> if you still disagree on the amount.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> can also help you see how replacement cost, depreciation, the deductible, and prior payments add up.</p>

<h2>Why Work With a Public Adjuster in Dallas</h2>
<p>We prepare our own Xactimate estimates and document damage with photos and Matterport 3D imaging where it helps, so our findings line up with how insurers write their estimates. Joseph Dittman’s background includes carrier-side adjusting and hands-on construction experience, which helps in reading both the estimate and the building. We represent the insured only, and we do not perform repairs on claims we adjust. A review cannot guarantee a different payment, and coverage depends on your policy. Fees for representation are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. To get a second look at your estimate, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
